ABOUT = f'''<h2>About Reversium</h2><p class="brand">Build {__version__} · <a href="https://3Sci.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">3Sci.com</a> · 2026</p>
<p>Reversium helps you understand an unfamiliar software package before you take responsibility for it. You give it a ZIP, and it examines the files on your computer to produce an organized report. You do not need to understand programming to start exploring the results.</p>
<h3>Where does Reversium fit?</h3><p>Use Reversium as a private first step for handover and maintenance discovery. Its combination of browser-local ZIP analysis, supported feature paths, evidence, unknowns and reviewer actions helps bridge software owners and developers. It complements specialist architecture, quality and security tools; it does not replace their depth. Core analysis needs no model, account or paid service.</p><h3>What can it help you achieve?</h3>
<p><b>Understand what you have received.</b> See which files, programming languages, frameworks and supporting services are referenced. This helps you ask informed questions when buying software, receiving a contractor’s work or inheriting an older project.</p>
<p><b>Find where a feature lives.</b> Supported source tracing can connect a screen action to a callback, an API request and a database operation. The map gives a developer a starting point for investigating a bug or planning a change. Every supported connection leads back to evidence, and inferred connections remain candidates.</p>
<p><b>Prepare a practical handover.</b> Use the report to identify the parts a new developer needs to understand, the services the software may require and the questions that still need answers. The report helps plan setup and maintenance; it does not automatically install or run the software.</p>
<p><b>Review a new delivery.</b> Keep a JSON baseline and compare another package to identify changes in inspected files and mapped references. Keep an HTML report as a readable record for colleagues.</p>
<p><b>Ask for an explanation.</b> The optional AI assistant can explain selected findings and suggest checks through your own eligible ChatGPT account. You review the question and evidence before sharing. Local analysis still works without AI, an account or an internet connection.</p>
<h3>A simple way to use it</h3><ol><li>Choose the software ZIP and analyze it locally.</li><li>Read Software findings, then explore Architecture and its feature paths.</li><li>Open Evidence to check where a finding came from. Read Coverage to understand what was not inspected or established.</li><li>Save a report and give a developer a short list of questions to confirm with isolated tests.</li></ol>
<h3>What the report cannot promise</h3><p>Finding a reference in code does not prove it runs successfully. Reversium cannot guarantee the complete purpose of an arbitrary program, identify every feature, certify security, prove production readiness or recover missing passwords, data and services. Binary inspection is limited metadata/reference inspection, not full decompilation. Real installation, permissions, business behavior and reliability need separate tests.</p>
<p>The practical benefit is a better starting point for a handover, maintenance investigation or modernization discussion, with evidence and gaps made visible. Local analysis is free; optional cloud AI follows your provider’s eligibility and usage limits.</p>'''

FAQ_GENERAL = '''<h2>FAQ</h2><details><summary>Who would use Reversium?</summary><p>A software owner checking a handover, a developer inheriting a project, or a team preparing maintenance or migration. Owners get clearer questions to ask; developers get source locations to investigate.</p></details><details><summary>Will it run the software for me?</summary><p>No. It inspects the archive without executing its code. Use a separate disposable environment for setup and runtime acceptance.</p></details><details><summary>Does a diagram prove a feature works?</summary><p>No. It shows supported source references and candidate connections. Conditions, configuration, permissions and external services can change what actually happens.</p></details>'''
